  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe approach leverages the periodic structure of optimal solutions to the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. We hypothesize that the optimal solution exhibits a repeating pattern with a specific period length. By optimizing a candidate pattern of fixed length and using it to construct a solution for the entire grid, we can focus the search on a lower-dimensional space while maintaining the integral constraint and the DC balance. This method is computationally efficient because we optimize a short sequence rather than the full grid, while still allowing for local improvements through iterative refinement. The rationale is that periodic patterns are likely to minimize maximum overlap due to their self-similar structure, and small variations around an optimal pattern parameterization may yield further improvement. The primary bottleneck this addresses is the vast search space of high-resolution grids, whereas our method works with small snippets and reduces evaluation cost.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- **Variables**: We use a binary signal representation at unit spacing, define a snippet (pattern) s \u2208 {0,1}^m, where m is the temporal length of the periodic component.\n- **Objective**: Minimize the maximum correlation between s and its circular shifts, i.e., minimize C = max_k ||correlation(s, 1-s, k) * dx||, where dx=2/n.\n- **Constraints**: Authors: bit_labels in {0,1}^m, sum(s)=m/2, and for the full gradient, the solution h \u2208 ([0,1]^n) matches a periodic extension of s.\n- **Periodicity relation**: The solution vector h has period m, so h(i) = s(i mod m) for all i.\n- **Integral balance**: The period's integral defines the grid occupancy floor(n/m)*integral_period + residual \u2248 1.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialize:\n      - Set period length m (e.g., m=8,12) and grid n (e.g., n=400).\n      - Generate an initial h0 for n by projecting s0 (initial snippet) to grid via periodic extension.\n      - Enforce integrality: adjust h0 to have integral 1 using gradient projection.\n   b. Basic periodic optimization:\n      - Optimize the snippet s \u2208 {0,1}^m to minimize user-defined objective via SLSQP (using constraints sum(s)=m/2).\n      - Use finite-differences to approximate gradient for minimal changes (step size=0.1).\n      - Apply 5000 iterations with max_active_pass=500 for convergence monitoring.\n   c. Ancillary refinements:\n      - Phase: up-sampling grid if time remains (n_new = max(n, 800)).\n      - Expanding period post-hoc if initial objective shows improvement potential.\n      - Redistributed blocks via cyclic shifts and runs-length encoding of active states.\n   d. Finalization:\n      - Down-project high-resolution solution to base grid if desired.\n      - Validate feasibility (sum(h)=n/2, 0<=h[i]<=1) via clipping and projection.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations \n   - Primary snippet length: m \u2208 {6,8,10,12,15}\n   - Initial snippets: parametric forms \u2192 \n        - Alternating: advection model (binary waveforms)\n        - Random with sum=0.5m\n        - Periodic register-shifts\n   - Optimization heuristics:\n        - Apply pattern-rotations from scratch if stuck in local min\n        - F$\\beta$-weighted objectives: $f(s)=\\max\\{C, -\\log(1-\\text{diversity}(s))\\}$ \n        - Strategy |frequency: intensification around peaks to gradient estimates\n   - Dimension scalability: vary m systematically (from small to large) to meet time constraint.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Core optimization: O(m) per evaluation, with m<20.\n   - Early termination: compute gradient-based step at every optimization step if score falls\n   - Fallback: periodic solution unless >3 resampled grid phases fail to improve\n   - Memory: store best-known snippet snapshots to restart from prior progress.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Compute C using provided `compute_c5` with n\n   - Retain 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 by projection\n   - Check integral: |sum(h) - n/2| < 1e-6 (use `project_feasible`)\n   - Return: best_h (float version), C(val), n_used\n   - Metrics: log(final_C - best_C_initial) \u2265 strict_threshold=0.000063",
